CAT = "inbdeMaterials"; SEG = "MAT"
# FK3: physics and chemistry of technologies and materials (radiology, materials, lasers).
Q = [
("Most of the x-ray photons produced in a dental tube come from which process?",
 "Bremsstrahlung radiation", ["Characteristic radiation", "Gamma emission from the target", "Compton scattering in the tube"],
 "Most photons arise as electrons slow down near tungsten nuclei, giving a continuous spectrum. Characteristic radiation is a small part of the beam, and Compton scatter happens in the patient, not the tube."),
("Raising the kVp while keeping other settings the same produces a beam with:",
 "Higher energy and lower contrast", ["Lower energy and higher contrast", "More photons at the same energy", "Less penetration and more contrast"],
 "Higher kVp gives more penetrating photons and a longer gray scale (lower contrast). Milliamperage changes the number of photons, not their energy."),
("Within the patient's tissues, which interaction of a diagnostic x-ray beam produces most of the scatter radiation?",
 "Compton scattering", ["Photoelectric absorption", "Coherent scattering", "Pair production"],
 "In the diagnostic energy range, Compton interactions eject an outer electron and send a weaker photon off in a new direction, the main source of scatter. Photoelectric absorption removes the photon and gives image contrast; pair production needs far higher energies."),
("If the distance from the source to the receptor is doubled and nothing else changes, the beam intensity becomes:",
 "One quarter as strong", ["One half as strong", "Twice as strong", "Unchanged in strength"],
 "By the inverse square law, intensity falls with the square of distance: twice the distance gives one over two squared, or one quarter."),
("Which phase of a low-copper dental amalgam is the weakest and least corrosion resistant?",
 "Gamma 2, tin-mercury", ["Gamma, silver-tin", "Gamma 1, silver-mercury", "Eta, copper-tin"],
 "The tin-mercury gamma 2 phase corrodes and weakens the set amalgam. High-copper alloys largely eliminate it by forming the copper-tin eta phase instead."),
("Which cavity preparation has the highest configuration factor (C-factor) for a bonded composite?",
 "A Class I occlusal preparation", ["A Class IV incisal angle", "A Class II with one proximal box", "A Class III with facial access"],
 "The C-factor is bonded walls divided by unbonded surfaces. A Class I box has five bonded walls and one free surface, so shrinkage stress is highest."),
("How does a light-cured resin-modified glass ionomer set?",
 "Acid-base reaction plus light curing", ["Light curing of resin alone", "Acid-base reaction alone", "Evaporation of the water carrier"],
 "Resin-modified glass ionomers keep the acid-base setting reaction of conventional glass ionomer and add a resin component that polymerizes with light (and often chemically), giving early strength."),
("Which all-ceramic material has the highest flexural strength for a posterior crown?",
 "Zirconia (yttria-stabilized)", ["Feldspathic porcelain", "Leucite-reinforced glass ceramic", "Lithium disilicate glass ceramic"],
 "Monolithic yttria-stabilized zirconia is the strongest dental ceramic. Lithium disilicate is strong but less so, and feldspathic and leucite ceramics are weaker still."),
("How is the fitting surface of a lithium disilicate crown prepared for adhesive cementation?",
 "Hydrofluoric acid etch, then silane", ["Phosphoric acid etch, then bonding", "Air abrasion with no primer", "MDP primer with no etching"],
 "Hydrofluoric acid dissolves the glass phase to create micromechanical retention and silane couples the ceramic to the resin. Zirconia, with no glass phase, is air-abraded and primed with MDP instead."),
("Which elastic impression material stays most dimensionally stable when the cast is poured days later?",
 "Polyvinyl siloxane", ["Irreversible hydrocolloid", "Condensation silicone", "Polysulfide rubber"],
 "Addition silicone sets with no by-product, so it changes little over time. Condensation silicone loses alcohol, polysulfide loses water and alginate loses or gains water within minutes."),
("What happens to set dental stone when more water is used in the mix?",
 "It becomes weaker", ["It becomes stronger", "It sets much faster", "It expands much more"],
 "Extra water leaves more porosity once it evaporates, so the cast is weaker. A thinner mix also sets more slowly and shows less setting expansion."),
("Erbium lasers can remove enamel and dentin because their wavelength is strongly absorbed by:",
 "Water and hydroxyapatite", ["Hemoglobin in blood only", "Melanin in pigmented tissue", "Titanium surfaces"],
 "Erbium wavelengths are absorbed by water and hydroxyapatite, so the water in hard tissue vaporizes and ablates it. Diode and Nd:YAG wavelengths target pigment and hemoglobin in soft tissue."),
("On a panoramic image, why do structures far from the focal trough look blurred?",
 "They lie outside the sharp layer", ["The beam is collimated too tightly", "The exposure time is too short", "The receptor is too sensitive"],
 "A panoramic unit records a curved layer (the focal trough) sharply. Objects in front of or behind it are magnified, narrowed or blurred, which is why head position matters."),
("Case: 30-year-old man. On a periapical image of tooth 8 the tooth looks much longer than it is. What caused this?",
 "Too little vertical angulation", ["Too much vertical angulation", "Wrong horizontal angulation", "Too long an exposure time"],
 "With the bisecting technique, too little vertical angulation elongates the image and too much foreshortens it. Horizontal errors overlap the contacts."),
("Case: 52-year-old man who grinds heavily needs a crown on tooth 30 with little space between the arches. Which crown needs the least occlusal reduction?",
 "Full cast gold alloy", ["Feldspathic porcelain", "Porcelain fused to metal", "Pressed lithium disilicate"],
 "Cast gold is strong in thin section, so it needs less occlusal reduction than ceramics or metal-ceramic crowns, which need room for porcelain thickness."),
("Case: 41-year-old woman with a Class V lesion at the gingival margin of tooth 6 that will be hard to keep dry. What is the main concern with placing a composite there?",
 "Moisture weakens the resin bond", ["Composite releases too much fluoride", "The lesion is too shallow to restore", "Composite cannot be shaded to match"],
 "Resin bonding is technique sensitive and fails with blood or saliva contamination, which is why isolation matters at cervical margins and why glass ionomer is often used there instead."),
]
